19-Aug-2015\tab P?S/8 PAL bug fix.\par
\par
Problem: The ASMIFM, ASMIFN and ASMIFZ directives are non-functional.\par
\par
Note: These directives are part of the dual mode assembly option and were added for compatibility with the DIAL-MS assembler.\par
\par
Detailed explanation.\par
\par
The total failure of the three directives mentioned above was traced to an incorrect offset past a symbolic reference within the source code due to gradual changes in the coding.\page\cf1 P?S/8 Version 8Z Change Log [continued].\par
\cf0\par
This is actually a poor programming technique that is no longer used in P?S/8 development.  If a specific location will be modified, it should have its own symbolic identification.  In this case, the referenced address is part of a relocation list.  [The dual mode assembly feature is one of the modular options; depending on the specific combination of modules retained, a dynamic update reflecting the relocation must be applied to all memory locations in the rest of the assembler coding that need to be updated accordingly.]  In this specific case, the relocation was correct; however, it was applied to an incorrect memory location.\line\line Resolution: As a temporary fix, the proper address will be patched to restore proper operation.  The source code will be modified to include a proper symbolic reference [without a potentially "drifting" offset] to ensure the accuract of the coding.  [At the source code level, the address requiring the relocation was erroneously coded as MASSIF+2 when in fact it should be MASSIF+4 due to changes in the source code over the years.  A new reference to NASSIF without an offset will be applied, as this is presently the closest symbolic address.]\par
\par
Implementation: Using P?S/8 BLKODT, the problem is readily fixed:\par
\par
.BLKODT\par
\par
0:0277.010 /2373 2375\par
S\par
.\par
\par
Note:  Conditional directives such as IFZERO, IFNZRO, IFDEF and IFNDEF are universally available in P?S/8 PAL regardless of modular options chosen [such as dual mode assembly].\par
\par
The DIAL assembler supports the ASMIFM, ASMIFN, ASMIFZ and ASMSKP directives as an equivalent functionality to the IFZERO and IFNZRO directives; however, the usage is somewhat more cumbersome [in part due to the usage of one's complement arithmetic when assembling in LINC mode].  The standard directives can handle equivalent parameters to achieve the same results, generally using a simpler overall approach.  [The IFDEF and IFNDEF directives have no functional equivalent in the DIAL assembler.]\par
\par
Since the standard directives are fully compatible with all popular PAL assemblers [such as TOPS-10 PAL10, OS/8 PAL8 and P?S/8 PAL], it is recommended to avoid the DIAL assembler directives in all new work.\par
\par
As such, support for the DIAL conditional directives will be available in P?S/8 PAL only as part of the dual mode assembly module.\par
\par
Additional P?S/8 PAL issues:\par
\par
No fix for the following problems will be made available for this release of P?S/8 PAL.  A minimal workaround is discussed below.\page\cf1 P?S/8 Version 8Z Change Log [continued].\par
\cf0\par
Certain directives are implemented in the dual mode assembly module that arguably should be available regardless of modular options set; the only way to use the directives is by enabling dual mode assembly with the /8 or /9 option switches.\par
\par
The ENBITS and NOBITS directives are available for compatibility with TOPS-10 PAL10; however, there is no actual function performed [as the feature within PAL10 the directives manage is not implemented in any other PAL assembler].  [The intended usage is to enable or disable the automatic bitmap dump after every FIELD directive usage.  OS/8 PAL8 and P?S/8 PAL do not support this implementation; however, both systems support an independent bitmap facility to perform an equivalent function on the binary output of the assembly.  As such, it is not likely that functional compatibility will be available in either system.]\par
\par
Since these directives are not particularly related to dual assembly mode, the next source-level release of P?S/8 PAL will shift these directives into the main symbol table load.  For the present, if these features are required, the restriction of requiring the /8 option switch be set will apply.\cf1\par
\cf0\par
The DTORG directive is also erroneously part of the dual mode assembly module; however, DTORG should not be used as it will tend to corrupt the binary output.  [There had been an intention to create an alternate special-purpose loading utility to punch binary paper-tapes of output containing DTORG statements, but as a practical matter, there no longer is any interest in the Typeset-8 product, the only system where this feature was ever required.  However, future releases of P?S/8 will provide an alternate implementation that is also fully compatible with all binary output standards.  At the point of release, the directive will be moved to the main symbol table load in the same manner as the NOBITS and ENBITS directives as discussed above.  Future P?S/8 system generation utilities will require the revamped DTORG directive; it will be possible to convert the compatible binary output to the format as required by Typeset-8 punched on incompatible binary paper tapes.]\par
